{:edb/db-before-t 846 :edb/db-after-t 847 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534159 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:19:57.028Z" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186156993 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm}$, distributed in the ratio $5:1$. Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}]$." 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186156993 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}].$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186156993 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$. Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm}$, and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1$. Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of\n\n$$\n5+1 = 6\n$$\n\nrings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ &= \\frac{1}{6}∑_(i)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(5 \\cdot 16.5 + 18) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(82.5 + 18) \\\\\n&= \\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\\n&= 16.75mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186156993 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu.$ Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $5+1 = 6$ rings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(5\\cdot 16.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(82.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\  & =16.75\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157172 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14 , \\quad 11, \\quad 7\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157172 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14, \\quad 11, \\quad 7$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186157172 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu$.\nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n$, while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$,\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu$, and\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ^(^) &= x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(7)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}(6 + 13 + 7 + 5 + 14 + 11 + 7) \\\\\n&= \\frac{63}{7} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186157172 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412752 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412754 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412755 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412757 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412760 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412753 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412756 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412758 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412759 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157528 1050 420.0 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153838 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186157599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412745 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157599 1071 "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6cm$ and $12cm$, distributed in the ratio $3: 1$. Calculate $E[X^(―)]$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186157599 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186157599 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,12\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(18+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{4} \\\\  & =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186158531 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu$, and\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2$.\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns.\n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu$. However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n1. Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n2. Count the number of pages in each book.\n3. Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5$.\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i$. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(201 + 506 + 58 + 104 + 99) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 968 \\\\\n&= 193.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value\n\n$$\n\\bar{x} = 193.6\n$$\n\nis called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu$. We sometimes write\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6\n$$\n\nto denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu$.\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186158531 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n- Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n- Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n- Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186158560 1064 "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables\n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n$\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n$\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}$.\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$, then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}$. The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}$. The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186158560 1064 "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n.\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}.$\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n,$ then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}.$ The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}.$ The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186158561 1064 "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu$. This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu$, that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^(―)] &= E[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (E[X_{1}] + E[X_{2}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (μ + μ + ⋯ + μ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nμ \\\\\n&= μ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons.\n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu$." 13194139534159 17592186045417 false] [17592186158561 1064 "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu.$ This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu,$ that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\mu  \\\\  & =\\mu \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons. \n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is calle