2 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412752 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n-8,\\,5,\\,-14,\\,-20,\\,16\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412752 1072 #uuid "1efe5ada-0d49-54bc-8531-cf777586f9d6" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412752 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-8+5-14-20+16) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{5} \\\\  & =-4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412743 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412753 1069 "q-149734" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412753 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{70}$ is a sample of size $n=70$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =21$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=2,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412753 1072 #uuid "8bcbbb4b-5f73-5c63-9997-c0acc2c3bd21" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412753 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =21,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=21.$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412742 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412754 1069 "q-88951" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412754 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n4,\\,-7,\\,6,\\,-3,\\,5,\\,-2\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412754 1072 #uuid "8d4c868b-7564-5dcc-a2d7-26fd40beb90e" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412754 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4-7+6-3+5-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 17592186045417 true] [17592186412755 1068 #edb/ref 175921864127